19
00:01:25,710 --> 00:01:30,380
That is, what is even sourcing in a few words? You really think that is a simple question? Many teams struggle with this.

20
00:01:30,390 --> 00:01:31,110
[laughter]

21
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:34,830
So, in essence many people start to look at it from a technical perspective.

22
00:01:34,830 --> 00:01:43,190
So, if you want to look at it from a technical perspective because we also have a technical audience, it's just a different way to store information and to process information.

23
00:01:43,190 --> 00:01:49,870
So, if you look at a typical CRUD architecture, what we're doing is we're just storing structured objects without any history.

24
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:57,590
And even sourcing turns this around a little bit and we just we store a whole history of things that happen in our system in the form of events.

25
00:01:57,590 --> 00:02:00,670
That's the only thing that event sourcing brings in.

26
00:02:00,670 --> 00:02:04,830
And this has some quite interesting effects on information processing and how you structure your whole system.

27
00:02:04,840 --> 00:02:06,950
Okay.

28
00:02:06,950 --> 00:02:15,710
Can you give me quickly just a small example maybe of system you worked on about this or just to give the audience an idea of what may look like system like this I mean. Yeah, of course.

29
00:02:15,710 --> 00:02:19,191
So, for example, I'm currently working on a system that processes law information. So, it's in the law space.

30
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:23,070
So, working a lot together with law agents.

31
00:02:23,070 --> 00:02:33,150
And what we do there is if you open a new case, instead of storing the typical case information in a table that is called cases, we just store a new event which is called case created for example.

32
00:02:33,150 --> 00:02:36,390
And whenever there is a new case in the system, there is a new event that is stored case created.

33
00:02:36,390 --> 00:02:40,630
And then you have a complete history of all those cases.

34
00:02:40,630 --> 00:02:44,350
And you can just look it up when was this case created, who created this case, all the information is stored.

35
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:46,710
Thank you.

36
00:02:46,710 --> 00:02:50,590
And the terminology, terminology around events in software can be really, really confusing.

37
00:02:50,590 --> 00:02:57,110
So, what are the relationships, if any, between event sourcing, event streaming, event storming, event driven?

38
00:02:57,110 --> 00:03:00,270
There is a lot of event modeling. That of course is also a term.

39
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:02,551
course, yeah. Yes, you're right.

40
00:03:02,551 --> 00:03:06,030
Yeah, the problem is actually I don't like the term at all because it's so bloated.

41
00:03:06,040 --> 00:03:11,310
Whenever you say event, you can ask two developers and you will get three answers at least.

42
00:03:11,310 --> 00:03:13,150
So, many people have a very different understanding.

43
00:03:13,150 --> 00:03:21,949
And also, as you mentioned, there is event streaming, there is event storming, all those different terminologies all around events and they mean very, very different things.

44
00:03:21,949 --> 00:03:26,430
So, first of all, when I say event, what I mean with this is not a technical term.

45
00:03:26,430 --> 00:03:28,790
An event is just something that happened in the system.

46
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:30,870
It's a fact that happened in the system.

47
00:03:30,880 --> 00:03:32,910
It's not a technical term at all.

48
00:03:32,910 --> 00:03:38,790
I'm really looking at information systems and I typically refer to software systems as information systems.

49
00:03:38,790 --> 00:03:41,670
I really look at them from a business perspective.

50
00:03:41,670 --> 00:03:45,030
So, an event is just something that happened in the system from a business perspective.

51
00:03:45,030 --> 00:03:48,030
Any business person can understand this immediately.

52
00:03:48,030 --> 00:03:50,830
Something that happened in the system, that's all. All that an event is.

53
00:03:50,840 --> 00:03:58,670
And now a bit of with event modeling because event sourcing and modeling as we said they are kind of strongly related.

54
00:03:58,670 --> 00:04:01,030
So, event modeling in a few words, what is that?

55
00:04:01,030 --> 00:04:05,310
Event modeling is the starting point of any information system.

56
00:04:05,310 --> 00:04:10,590
So, many of the listeners most probably are familiar with event storming which is very similar to event modeling.

57
00:04:10,590 --> 00:04:16,550
Event modeling has just some specialties that are different to event storming, but in essence it's a collaborative modeling technique.

58
00:04:16,550 --> 00:04:26,190
And the idea of event storming and also event modeling is you bring people together very early in the process, very early in the project also, and they start to discuss things.

59
00:04:26,190 --> 00:04:35,909
You start to discuss those problems because one thing I realized a few years ago and that changed my whole career was that the problems we are facing in most of our systems,

60
00:04:35,909 --> 00:04:41,909
in most projects, and I've been in the industry for over 20 years, and all those projects I was involved with, they faced the same problems.

61
00:04:41,909 --> 00:04:43,510
It was always communication.

62
00:04:43,510 --> 00:04:45,671
It was never technology. It was always communication.

63
00:04:45,680 --> 00:04:54,630
When you bring people together very early in the project and you make them talk to each other and really talk about the problems, many of those problems they simply seem to fade away.

64
00:04:54,630 --> 00:05:00,750
Because when you make people talk to each other and they really find this common terminology, this common language, everything seems to work suddenly.

65
00:05:00,750 --> 00:05:02,590
And this is almost magical.

66
00:05:02,590 --> 00:05:07,550
And event modeling, event storming, and all those collaborative modeling techniques, they do just that.

67
00:05:07,550 --> 00:05:15,310
Basically they enforce good communication in the project, especially together with event sourcing, that's just a great combination to build information systems.

68
00:05:15,310 --> 00:05:16,909
Okay, and so what is the relationship with event sourcing?

69
00:05:16,919 --> 00:05:26,751
We can follow what you said, event modeling seems to be some level of I don't know, in between requirements and design maybe, high-level design, not technical design of the system. Exactly.

70
00:05:26,760 --> 00:05:33,150
So, what you do basically with event modeling is you just describe an information system from beginning to end.

71
00:05:33,150 --> 00:05:37,350
The big difference to event storming is in event modeling you do it along one single timeline.

72
00:05:37,350 --> 00:05:39,270
So, it's like you're telling a story.

73
00:05:39,270 --> 00:05:47,110
You're finding the story of a system together with engineering, together with the business experts, and it's always readable from left to right.

74
00:05:47,110 --> 00:05:50,590
And that's the amazing thing because everybody knows how to read from left to right.

75
00:05:50,590 --> 00:06:03,070
So, in an event model you can start on the left side and if you want to know how a certain business process works, you can just go from left to right step by step because everything in the event model is laid down step by step and you can understand what happens in the system step by step.

76
00:06:03,080 --> 00:06:09,550
Basically, it's like a prototype. You can go through it like a prototype and you see exactly screen by screen what is going on in the system.

77
00:06:09,550 --> 00:06:15,670
And if you look at that, it's a perfect blueprint to an event-driven system and also to an event source system.

78
00:06:15,670 --> 00:06:23,390
So, especially when you work with modeling there is a very easy mapping between your event model and the event source system.

79
00:06:23,390 --> 00:06:25,150
It's so simple that you can even use it to generate code.

80
00:06:25,160 --> 00:06:27,870
This is what I'm doing a lot. I'm generating a lot of code.

81
00:06:27,870 --> 00:06:30,270
So, I'm sitting together with clients.

82
00:06:30,270 --> 00:06:33,990
We model their processes, try to understand their problems.

83
00:06:33,990 --> 00:06:37,270
And when we did that, we use the event model directly to generate code from this.

84
00:06:37,270 --> 00:06:40,470
And this really, really interesting, really interesting way to work.

85
00:06:40,470 --> 00:06:47,990
One thing that you wrote in your book, you said that one of the most interesting things about event sourcing is that it's natural alignment with human thinking.

86
00:06:47,990 --> 00:06:50,310
What do you mean with that?

87
00:06:50,310 --> 00:06:54,150
When you develop systems without event sourcing, so what happens when you get a problem as a typical developer?

88
00:06:54,160 --> 00:06:58,550
You sit down and the first thing a developer thinks of is, "Okay, what is the tables that I need?"

89
00:06:58,550 --> 00:07:00,510
Oh, let's take a simple example.

90
00:07:00,510 --> 00:07:02,510
I need to build a user registration.

91
00:07:02,510 --> 00:07:11,830
Almost every developer will immediately think of a user and of a users table and an address table and the typical structural things you see in the system.

92
00:07:11,830 --> 00:07:15,590
What they don't do is they don't talk about the processes behind that.

93
00:07:15,590 --> 00:07:17,110
But then you go to business, they never think about tables.

94
00:07:17,110 --> 00:07:20,630
They never think about users and those structured classes you have in your system.

95
00:07:20,630 --> 00:07:22,870
They think in processes.

96
00:07:22,870 --> 00:07:35,071
And when you use event modeling and also event sourcing, you start to think in processes because when you think in events, it's always, "Oh, this happened and then this happened and then the user was registered and then this notification was sent." Very natural.

97
00:07:35,071 --> 00:07:41,470
Basically, you can tell the story in natural language and this can directly be mapped to an event source system because that's exactly what happens there.

98
00:07:41,470 --> 00:07:44,070
Going to event sourcing more data now.

99
00:07:44,070 --> 00:07:47,150
So, of course there are several parts we can now discuss them all.

100
00:07:47,150 --> 00:07:50,990
When we talk about internal external events, event stores, projections, and other things.

101
00:07:50,990 --> 00:07:53,350
Oh yeah, all those technicalities, yes.

102
00:07:53,350 --> 00:07:55,190
We really have to talk about those.

103
00:07:55,190 --> 00:08:06,030
But, I'd like to start with the core patterns because again, reading your book followed by Adam Dmytruk, he wrote, "Event sourcing is not only revolutionary, but also responsible."

104
00:08:06,030 --> 00:08:08,710
With just two core patterns, it was remarkably simple.

105
00:08:08,710 --> 00:08:11,030
So, what are these two core patterns?

106
00:08:11,040 --> 00:08:14,270
Actually, I refer to them as four patterns.

107
00:08:14,270 --> 00:08:19,430
Adam uses constant two patterns, but I use four patterns because for me that is simpler to explain.

108
00:08:19,430 --> 00:08:21,470
So, in essence, that's what you do when you use event modeling.

109
00:08:21,470 --> 00:08:25,751
You break the system down into tiny little steps. We call them slices.

110
00:08:25,751 --> 00:08:32,589
So, basically what you have in the end is a slice-based architecture, but you break them down into slices.

111
00:08:32,589 --> 00:08:34,430
And there are basically just two types of slices.

112
00:08:34,430 --> 00:08:37,670
The first slice type is state change slice.

113
00:08:37,680 --> 00:08:40,909
State change just means how does information get into the system?

114
00:08:40,909 --> 00:08:43,870
There are just two ways to get information into the system.

115
00:08:43,870 --> 00:08:46,030
Typically, it's a screen.

116
00:08:46,030 --> 00:08:54,310
You have a screen, a user clicks on a button on the screen, fires a command into the system, and if everything goes well, there's a new event in the system, a new information that is stored in the system.

117
00:08:54,310 --> 00:08:56,030
That's a state change slice.

118
00:08:56,030 --> 00:08:57,829
Basically, just getting information into the system.

119
00:08:57,829 --> 00:09:01,990
And the second slice type is a state fuse slice.

120
00:09:01,990 --> 00:09:09,311
So, you use these events which are already available in the system, and you provide them for the next screen, you provide them for the next background process. That's all.

121
00:09:09,311 --> 00:09:12,190
Information getting into the system, information getting out of the system.

122
00:09:12,190 --> 00:09:23,270
And if you break the system down into those patterns, suddenly it becomes very simple because every system in the world, no matter how big it is, no matter how complicated it is, can be broken down into state changes and state fuses.

123
00:09:23,270 --> 00:09:27,070
And if you put them together like LEGO bricks, that's basically your system.

124
00:09:27,070 --> 00:09:37,990
And breaking those systems down into those tiny little steps makes it really easy to reason about even the most complex business processes because most problems, if you break them down, they become really simple.

125
00:09:37,990 --> 00:09:41,870
and it's really amazing, and that's one of my biggest learnings.

126
00:09:41,870 --> 00:09:44,070
Almost everything we do in software development isn't complex.

127
00:09:44,070 --> 00:09:47,790
Most of the things we do is rather simple if you just break it down.

128
00:09:47,790 --> 00:09:51,790
They just look so complicated if you look at those big problems.

129
00:09:51,790 --> 00:09:59,030
And if you have the stories in your estimations that last for 1,000 person days, well, that looks intimidating, and that looks really problematic.

130
00:09:59,030 --> 00:10:02,910
But if you break it down, suddenly it's just 1,000 tiny steps in this process.

131
00:10:02,910 --> 00:10:05,391
And every tiny step is very simple in and of itself. Okay.

132
00:10:05,391 --> 00:10:08,150
And now, let's go about the other parts.

133
00:10:08,150 --> 00:10:12,110
So, again, maybe you can start telling us if all the technicalities here.

134
00:10:12,110 --> 00:10:16,430
So, talking about the events, the type of events, the event store.

135
00:10:16,430 --> 00:10:20,591
So, tell us well, maybe a story at this parts and connect them together. How Yeah.

136
00:10:20,591 --> 00:10:26,551
the connecting them together so we understand how it is even sourcing is actually made here, is composed of. Yeah.

137
00:10:26,551 --> 00:10:33,270
Maybe we can put in a tiny little bit of event modeling here before we dive into event sourcing because it makes it easier to understand where this leads.

138
00:10:33,270 --> 00:10:36,910
So, if you do an event modeling workshop, it follows seven steps.

139
00:10:36,910 --> 00:10:40,790
So, in the end, what we try to do is we try to map out the system step by step.

140
00:10:40,790 --> 00:10:44,030
And the first thing we do typically is what's called the brainstorming.

141
00:10:44,030 --> 00:10:47,110
There we we gather all the people who are involved in the project.

142
00:10:47,110 --> 00:10:50,670
So, in the first step, brainstorming, many people can participate in the workshop.

143
00:10:50,670 --> 00:10:52,270
Can be 30 people.

144
00:10:52,270 --> 00:10:53,910
I had event modeling workshops with 30 people, and I do this a lot.

145
00:10:53,910 --> 00:10:58,870
I do a lot of those workshops for companies to help them get into event modeling and also into event sourcing.

146
00:10:58,880 --> 00:11:02,110
And the first part of the workshop is always brainstorming.

147
00:11:02,110 --> 00:11:04,510
That means you gather all the events.

148
00:11:04,510 --> 00:11:06,790
Just like putting sticky notes on a giant whiteboard.

149
00:11:06,790 --> 00:11:12,110
And this can be 100, this can be 500 events depending on how many people participate.

150
00:11:12,110 --> 00:11:16,510
But the interesting thing is what we're already doing here is we are shaping the ubiquitous language.

151
00:11:16,510 --> 00:11:22,630
And every developer who's listening now will immediately make the reference to domain-driven design, and this is exactly what we're doing there.

152
00:11:22,630 --> 00:11:25,590
It's always difficult to find the ubiquitous language.

153
00:11:25,590 --> 00:11:32,190
Ubiquitous language is basically the one language we are having in the project that everybody understands.

154
00:11:32,190 --> 00:11:36,310
Engineers, business people, security, everybody talks the same language.

155
00:11:36,310 --> 00:11:38,470
When I say customer, everybody knows what that means.

156
00:11:38,470 --> 00:11:40,390
In most projects, that is absolutely not the case.

157
00:11:40,390 --> 00:11:42,230
We have different terms for the same thing.

158
00:11:42,230 --> 00:11:45,190
You say one thing and everybody understands something different.

159
00:11:45,190 --> 00:11:47,070
One of the biggest problems we have in our project.

160
00:11:47,070 --> 00:11:57,870
But when you bring those people together and you just make them put those terms, those events on a board, suddenly those people start to discuss, sometimes for the first time in their life, what is the right naming for this?

161
00:11:57,870 --> 00:11:59,590
Can we find a common terminology for this?

162
00:11:59,590 --> 00:12:02,990
And this is where we already start to shape the ubiquitous language.

163
00:12:02,990 --> 00:12:08,230
The next thing that we do is we try to bring them in an order. This is where we form the story of the whole system.

164
00:12:08,230 --> 00:12:10,110
I call it storyboarding.

165
00:12:10,110 --> 00:12:13,510
It's just you take this whole cloud of events and you try to bring them in an order.

166
00:12:13,510 --> 00:12:16,230
This is where we try to find the story of the system.

167
00:12:16,240 --> 00:12:18,990
What happens one event after the other.

168
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:26,670
And just looking at the result of the storyboarding is very interesting because you can already see first, how big is the system?

169
00:12:26,670 --> 00:12:28,590
And second, what happens one step after the other.

170
00:12:28,590 --> 00:12:32,830
And this already gives us an indication, okay, what is the first thing that we need to tackle?

171
00:12:32,830 --> 00:12:34,750
What is the second thing that we need to tackle?

172
00:12:34,750 --> 00:12:37,230
The story is really becomes visible only after that.

173
00:12:37,240 --> 00:12:38,990
And this can be after 30 minutes.

174
00:12:38,990 --> 00:12:46,270
So, storyboarding and the brainstorm, this can be just 30 minutes and we already start to to map out what happens in the system.

175
00:12:46,270 --> 00:12:48,190
And the next thing that we do in the event modeling is we draw screens.

176
00:12:48,200 --> 00:12:54,590
Screens, also very, very important part and this is a key differentiator to event storming also.

177
00:12:54,590 --> 00:12:57,110
We heavily draw screens.

178
00:12:57,110 --> 00:13:03,150
And screens is really important because this allows everybody in the project to understand what we are discussing.

179
00:13:03,150 --> 00:13:11,950
So, if you leave out those screens and I see this a lot in workshops, people are confused because business people, they are not used to think in events and many, many engineers are also not used to think in events.

180
00:13:11,960 --> 00:13:15,270
Thinking in events, it's a different thing.

181
00:13:15,270 --> 00:13:24,791
If you've been in the CRUD world and you've developed CRUD based systems for 20 years and you learned from scratch building CRUD based systems, thinking in events is all but natural to you. And many people struggle with this.

182
00:13:24,800 --> 00:13:28,270
But if you bring in screens to the story, suddenly everything makes sense.

183
00:13:28,280 --> 00:13:36,990
That's really you have something visual and you can map the screen to the events and that seems to work for everybody and I see this very very often.

184
00:13:36,990 --> 00:13:42,270
Well, it's really almost a visual prototype of what the system may look like even if it is not the final design or anything.

185
00:13:42,270 --> 00:13:45,991
It's just about maybe the flows or the way it works. Yes.

186
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:51,550
But it gives people an anchor in their heads on how the system works, I guess, and helps Yes.

187
00:13:51,560 --> 00:13:53,670
Okay.

188
00:13:53,670 --> 00:13:57,110
And many people try to skip this because they think, "Oh, it's so time intense to draw those screens.

189
00:13:57,110 --> 00:13:58,870
I don't want to do this."

190
00:13:58,870 --> 00:14:00,710
I have a very simple rule.

191
00:14:00,710 --> 00:14:04,510
If it takes you more than 2 minutes to draw a screen, you're absolutely doing it wrong.

192
00:14:04,510 --> 00:14:07,990
2 minutes is the absolute maximum you must take to draw a screen.

193
00:14:07,990 --> 00:14:12,390
So, in essence, if you do event modeling, drawing ugly screens is a key skill you need to master.

194
00:14:12,390 --> 00:14:14,110
My screens are the worst.

195
00:14:14,110 --> 00:14:15,870
They're super ugly and I'm really proud of that.

196
00:14:15,870 --> 00:14:20,070
So, I can draw very good screens, very ugly, but very efficient.

197
00:14:20,070 --> 00:14:23,230
The key thing why we need those screens is you need to understand what does the user see?

198
00:14:23,230 --> 00:14:25,111
Is there a button? Is there an input field?

199
00:14:25,120 --> 00:14:26,630
What is displayed?

200
00:14:26,630 --> 00:14:30,110
It doesn't matter if it's a radio button or a checkbox or something like that.

201
00:14:30,110 --> 00:14:32,191
UX people hate this. They hate this in the beginning.

202
00:14:32,200 --> 00:14:38,750
Because they want to discuss, of course, the pixel-perfect screen designs, but this is absolutely not what this is about.

203
00:14:38,750 --> 00:14:40,790
So, it's really like, "Okay, let's break this down step by step."

204
00:14:40,790 --> 00:14:46,630
And what we see there already is we see the slices slowly forming. Slices are the building blocks in the system.

205
00:14:46,630 --> 00:14:48,590
What are the screens?

206
00:14:48,590 --> 00:14:50,150
What are the events that we have step by step?

207
00:14:50,150 --> 00:14:54,390
They're already you can see how the event-driven and event-sourced system slowly forms.

208
00:14:54,390 --> 00:15:00,630
And the next thing that you do then is when we have the screens and the events, we just define what are the commands and what are the read models.

209
00:15:00,630 --> 00:15:05,550
Now, we come a little bit more into the terminology that goes in the direction of event sourcing.

210
00:15:05,550 --> 00:15:08,751
So, commands and read models are basically just two important elements. Like, what is a command?

211
00:15:08,751 --> 00:15:12,910
A command is basically just an instruction to the system.

212
00:15:12,910 --> 00:15:14,750
You want the system to do something.

213
00:15:14,750 --> 00:15:23,750
So, register user, for example, could be a command that you send to the system and you want this user to be registered. You don't know if it works. You don't know what business rules are behind it. You just send command to the system.

214
00:15:23,750 --> 00:15:25,430
I want this user to be registered.

215
00:15:25,430 --> 00:15:28,190
If everything goes well, you have a new event in the system.

216
00:15:28,190 --> 00:15:35,350
But of course, there's a lot of validation going on because typically every command that we send to the system is backed by a whole lot of business rules.

217
00:15:35,350 --> 00:15:39,870
But if everything goes well, a successful command execution means there is a new event in the system.

218
00:15:39,870 --> 00:15:45,790
But then, if we progress in the story, so in the event model we go from left to right, there's a next screen that comes.

219
00:15:45,790 --> 00:15:49,430
And this next screen shows a list of registered users.

220
00:15:49,430 --> 00:15:53,150
Now you need to show well, where does this list of users come from?

221
00:15:53,150 --> 00:15:57,230
And there you just use the information that is already available in the system.

222
00:15:57,230 --> 00:15:59,510
It's the events, and you project them to the screen.

223
00:15:59,510 --> 00:16:01,391
How do you do a projection? That's a read model.

224
00:16:01,391 --> 00:16:03,310
In event model, we call this a read model.

225
00:16:03,320 --> 00:16:11,830
It's just a green sticky note that shows you this is the information that is displayed on the screen, and these are the events that provide this information.

226
00:16:11,830 --> 00:16:15,270
So what we are doing with event modeling is we call this information completeness.

227
00:16:15,270 --> 00:16:22,750
So for every step in the system, you need to be very sure where does this information come from, so you cannot make any wrong assumptions.

228
00:16:22,750 --> 00:16:26,071
What is the one of the biggest problems you face in most projects? It's wrong assumptions.

229
00:16:26,071 --> 00:16:28,829
You just assume information to be available.

230
00:16:28,839 --> 00:16:36,950
And then later, when you go into the implementation, then you realize, and that's very late in the project, then you realize, oh, some of our assumptions, they weren't right.

231
00:16:36,950 --> 00:16:46,070
That's a problem because you already allocated the budget, you already had the project plan, you already planned your sprints, you are ready to go into coding, but now you realize well, we cannot do this.

232
00:16:46,070 --> 00:16:50,630
And this is where it gets really, really expensive because now you need to do the re-planning.

233
00:16:50,630 --> 00:16:55,111
All the things you did before was basically just a waste of time. Event modeling turns this around.

234
00:16:55,120 --> 00:16:58,790
We ask those questions in the very beginning of the project, very early.

235
00:16:58,790 --> 00:17:06,949
So if there is a gap in our requirements, an email is not available, information is not available, we know from the beginning because you see it in the event model.

236
00:17:06,949 --> 00:17:10,309
You cannot map the event model if the information is not available.

237
00:17:10,309 --> 00:17:14,470
So for every step basically, we break it down and we know is the information available or not.

238
00:17:14,470 --> 00:17:21,670
And to build those screens, for example, you need a read model, and back to the terminology of event sourcing, that's basically called a projection.

239
00:17:21,670 --> 00:17:27,110
You use these events in the system and you simply project them to the information you need to display the screen.

240
00:17:27,110 --> 00:17:32,870
In your book you say that using event sourcing we are adding the dimension of time to our system.

241
00:17:32,870 --> 00:17:38,030
So, the system records the facts that happen along a timeline, which you said with events at least, things that happen.

242
00:17:38,030 --> 00:17:43,951
You said this dimension gets lost when data is flattened to a relational database schema. Tell us more about that.

243
00:17:43,951 --> 00:17:46,870
Let's go back to this example of the user registration.

244
00:17:46,870 --> 00:17:56,631
So, what you have typically in a traditional architecture is just have a table with user information, but you have to absolutely no history how this information got in there. Was this user registered?

245
00:17:56,631 --> 00:17:58,750
Was this registered by an automatic process?

246
00:17:58,760 --> 00:18:01,990
Was this done manually? Was this done by an administrator?

247
00:18:01,990 --> 00:18:04,110
How often was this user changed?

248
00:18:04,110 --> 00:18:06,710
All this information is lost typically.

249
00:18:06,710 --> 00:18:15,390
If you need to know what happened to this user, if you are lucky, you can get into the logs or into your temporal tables and find out what happened to this user, but from an architectural perspective,

250
00:18:15,390 --> 00:18:19,590
this information gets flattened and it's lost.

251
00:18:19,590 --> 00:18:21,590
And event sourcing is the exact opposite of that.

252
00:18:21,590 --> 00:18:27,990
The timeline, the information, how you got to a certain point in the system, it's an essential part of the architecture.

253
00:18:27,990 --> 00:18:36,190
So, you have everything recorded, every decision you made in the system, step by step, and basically since the beginning of your system.

254
00:18:36,190 --> 00:18:38,190
So, I have clients who work with event sourcing for 10 and more years.

255
00:18:38,200 --> 00:18:41,550
They have tons and tons of information in their databases.

256
00:18:41,550 --> 00:18:45,510
Every decision ever made in the last 10 years is recorded as a fact, as an event in the system.

257
00:18:45,520 --> 00:18:49,550
That's informational gold that they have there.

258
00:18:49,550 --> 00:18:55,910
So, everything is recorded and basically you throw this away intentionally if you just store the information and not the history behind it.

259
00:18:55,910 --> 00:18:57,830
And I will never understand why you would do this intentionally.

260
00:18:57,830 --> 00:19:00,430
For me this makes no sense, not at all.

261
00:19:00,430 --> 00:19:04,190
I can say in relational database you can store some of that information surely.

262
00:19:04,190 --> 00:19:13,670
When somebody registers a user you can put this, you know, it's been automated or somebody else, but if there are changes during time on this information, that that becomes a bit more difficult.

263
00:19:13,680 --> 00:19:22,830
Sometimes there is some versioning of stuff or versioning of tables, but that becomes a bit more difficult in a relational database to keep track of.

264
00:19:22,840 --> 00:19:26,350
Oftentimes what you do is, of course, companies need this information.

265
00:19:26,350 --> 00:19:30,550
So, what they do is they need to work around this.

266
00:19:30,550 --> 00:19:34,110
So, you work with temporal tables which store information along a timeline, but it's not really usable.

267
00:19:34,120 --> 00:19:40,550
You cannot work with this information. It's just there, but typically when I ask a client, "How often did you use your temporal tables?"

268
00:19:40,550 --> 00:19:42,310
The answer is never because it doesn't work.

269
00:19:42,310 --> 00:19:46,070
Same question, "How often did you use your backup?"

270
00:19:46,070 --> 00:19:48,031
Well, never because it doesn't work. Well, why are you doing then?

271
00:19:48,031 --> 00:19:49,870
It's always the same thing.

272
00:19:49,870 --> 00:19:54,390
Yeah, it is definitely more difficult to keep that information in a reasonable manner.

273
00:19:54,390 --> 00:20:02,270
But, also another thing then, well, a question after this one because we say we store every single decision and forever, you know, 10 years and more.

274
00:20:02,280 --> 00:20:06,950
But, then how do you manage this information if it starts to become big?

275
00:20:06,960 --> 00:20:12,551
If you start to have a lot of information, a lot of data in your databases. What do you do?

276
00:20:12,551 --> 00:20:17,671
Is there any form of archiving of events, deletion of old events? I don't know.

277
00:20:17,671 --> 00:20:27,190
Yeah, so of course, I often discuss with clients who want to learn about event sourcing, who want to understand event sourcing, and those are the first questions that typically come.

278
00:20:27,190 --> 00:20:39,030
Wait a second, if you store every decision, this will get crazy because in a crud-based system we simply overwrite the information and then it's gone and so we have very clear picture of how big this data is that we store.

279
00:20:39,030 --> 00:20:40,871
In event sourcing it's basically unlimited. How should we deal with that?

280
00:20:40,880 --> 00:20:47,270
But, first of all, if you store events, it doesn't get so big typically if you do it right.

281
00:20:47,270 --> 00:20:52,950
So, it's not like you're storing millions and millions of events that you need to process to get to a certain state.

282
00:20:52,950 --> 00:20:55,390
That's typically not what happens.

283
00:20:55,390 --> 00:20:57,990
So, oftentimes what you do is you store information per process.

284
00:20:57,990 --> 00:21:01,350
And a process itself isn't infinitely large.

285
00:21:01,360 --> 00:21:05,870
Your processes are typically very small and contained.

286
00:21:05,870 --> 00:21:09,670
So, what you have is you have streams of events and those streams of events they are typically very small.

287
00:21:09,680 --> 00:21:14,990
So if you want to know what happened to this A question, when we say process we mean what?

288
00:21:14,990 --> 00:21:17,350
A software process running or a business process?

289
00:21:17,350 --> 00:21:19,430
I'm specifically talking about the business process.

290
00:21:19,430 --> 00:21:24,390
So you really look at the business processes and typically you have the history of your customer.

291
00:21:24,390 --> 00:21:27,710
A customer was registered and a few more things that happened to this customer.

292
00:21:27,710 --> 00:21:33,030
That's one stream of events that you store in your database or that you store in your event store.

293
00:21:33,030 --> 00:21:35,670
But the history of this customer isn't infinitely large.

294
00:21:35,670 --> 00:21:40,111
Maybe it's 10, 15, 20, 30 events or maybe it's 100 events. It doesn't really matter.

295
00:21:40,111 --> 00:21:47,510
All those processes they're typically very small and typically it doesn't happen that you have to process millions of events to get to a certain state.

296
00:21:47,510 --> 00:21:52,430
If you do this typically there's a problem in the modeling or in how you structure your information.

297
00:21:52,430 --> 00:21:56,030
It shouldn't be the case.

298
00:21:56,030 --> 00:21:58,310
And so, how many of these streams are typical event source systems?

299
00:21:58,310 --> 00:22:01,110
So that is a several streams.

300
00:22:01,110 --> 00:22:05,550
It depends on the number of your business processes in the system.

301
00:22:05,550 --> 00:22:07,430
So it doesn't really matter how many streams you have.

302
00:22:07,430 --> 00:22:11,510
So typically you just define those streams alongside your business processes.

303
00:22:11,510 --> 00:22:15,550
So if there is a customer registration that is a dedicated stream.

304
00:22:15,550 --> 00:22:19,470
If there is a card system that you have in your e-commerce system that might be a dedicated stream.

305
00:22:19,480 --> 00:22:23,350
A card session might be a dedicated stream.

306
00:22:23,350 --> 00:22:24,950
So it's really you design your processes.

307
00:22:24,950 --> 00:22:28,550
If you have events that belong together to a business process that typically also belongs to a stream.

308
00:22:28,560 --> 00:22:31,750
And then there come a few other terminologies.

309
00:22:31,750 --> 00:22:39,870
So typically then you talk about aggregates or if you talk about newer concepts in the event sourcing that's DCB but we can talk about this a little bit later.

310
00:22:39,870 --> 00:22:44,630
And also in these streams, so if you have several streams, can these streams be dependent on each other?

311
00:22:44,630 --> 00:22:49,232
Are there any dependencies that that you would need to manage? It can is of course. So it can.

312
00:22:49,240 --> 00:22:52,830
So typically what you try to do is you try to contain those streams.

313
00:22:52,830 --> 00:22:57,630
So what you try to do is you try to keep those streams consistent.

314
00:22:57,630 --> 00:22:59,310
Within a stream you only want to have consistent data.

315
00:22:59,310 --> 00:23:02,710
Same as you try to have consistent data in your traditional architectures.

316
00:23:02,710 --> 00:23:04,550
It's the same idea.

317
00:23:04,550 --> 00:23:11,990
And if you build an event source system, what you nowadays have typically is you have an aggregate and this aggregate is basically a protection for your stream.

318
00:23:11,990 --> 00:23:19,511
So, if you send a command to your system, the first thing that happens typically is something in your system just checks, can I execute this command? Is this valid?

319
00:23:19,511 --> 00:23:22,750
If I execute this command, will my system be in a consistent state?

320
00:23:22,750 --> 00:23:25,830
And the thing that does this typically is the aggregate.

321
00:23:25,830 --> 00:23:29,830
It just sits basically on your stream and checks, can I execute this command or not?

322
00:23:29,830 --> 00:23:41,630
And typically, what you try to do is you try to keep those streams self-contained as much as possible, but of course, there can be dependencies between streams, but you try to keep them as consistent as possible in themselves.

323
00:23:41,630 --> 00:23:45,190
And when there are dependencies, how do you manage those?

324
00:23:45,190 --> 00:23:49,390
I'm thinking from the perspective of if you want to rebuild the state of the system at certain point in time.

325
00:23:49,390 --> 00:23:57,350
If you have these dependencies, how do you manage these different dependencies across streams to have a consistent state of your whole system?

326
00:23:57,350 --> 00:24:07,030
That's a very good question and this is where many teams really struggle because oftentimes, so what teams come up with when they reach that state is the the saga pattern.

327
00:24:07,030 --> 00:24:16,190
The saga pattern is basically how to define long-running processes and keep the system consistent even if it's across streams or even across different systems.

328
00:24:16,190 --> 00:24:17,791
I never used that in my systems. I don't need this.

329
00:24:17,800 --> 00:24:21,790
What we do in event modeling basically is we call this automations.

330
00:24:21,790 --> 00:24:30,070
It's basically just another process step that is responsible to use the information in one stream and send it to another stream.

331
00:24:30,070 --> 00:24:35,710
That's basically, you could call it a tiny little saga, but typically, I neither use the term saga pattern nor do I use this concept in my systems.

332
00:24:35,710 --> 00:24:37,630
I don't need it.

333
00:24:37,630 --> 00:24:44,750
But there's a small piece of automation or a small piece of software that just uses information from one stream and knows how to transform this information to another stream.

334
00:24:44,750 --> 00:24:48,911
And that's how you manage those dependencies typically. Okay.

335
00:24:48,911 --> 00:24:52,510
In terms of performances because surely you'll have this question all the time.

336
00:24:52,510 --> 00:24:59,710
Are there any implications in terms of latency, the time, memory, any performance implications on these systems.

337
00:24:59,710 --> 00:25:06,830
So, that's of course one of the biggest question that comes up because many people have a very wrong understanding what event sourcing actually means.

338
00:25:06,830 --> 00:25:19,350
So, if I talk to developers, what they think is, well, if I want to know what my customer looks like, every time I want to know this, I go to my event store and I read all the events of this customer basically every time I need this information.

339
00:25:19,350 --> 00:25:21,150
That is typically not what happens.

340
00:25:21,150 --> 00:25:25,910
So, what you have in event sourcing, and I use this a lot, is just persisted projections.

341
00:25:25,910 --> 00:25:29,390
So, at runtime it behaves exactly like every other traditional architecture as well.

342
00:25:29,390 --> 00:25:36,950
So, what happens basically whenever there is a new event, this gets persisted to a persisted projection, and this can be a relational table, for example.

343
00:25:36,950 --> 00:25:40,230
So, at runtime what you do is you read your customer's table.

344
00:25:40,230 --> 00:25:47,270
It's still your customer's table, but it's not your source of truth, the customer's table, but it's just a projection of the events of this customer.

345
00:25:47,270 --> 00:25:53,790
And whenever you want to change this, or you need new information from this customer, you can just build a new projection or two new projections of this.

346
00:25:53,790 --> 00:25:57,550
You are very, very flexible in how you work with your information.

347
00:25:57,550 --> 00:26:07,870
Okay, so if I'm understanding correctly, you have events that you store somewhere, and then they are facts, things that happened to a data, but then every time an event happens,

348
00:26:07,870 --> 00:26:15,470
we can update, say, table in a relational database, which is basically the most recent view of that particular data set, as a customer, as you said.

349
00:26:15,480 --> 00:26:20,591
Exactly. Exactly. This can be like a snapshot. Yes.

350
00:26:20,591 --> 00:26:24,670
And so, if I want to see that at a past time, what I need to do is rebuild that view, but up to a point.

351
00:26:24,680 --> 00:26:33,111
So, not up to the most recent event, but maybe an event, I don't know, 10 days ago, because if I'm interested to data in the past, I do that. Yes.

352
00:26:33,111 --> 00:26:37,590
For example, if you want to do that, if you need to And I do this a lot for bug fixing, for example.

353
00:26:37,590 --> 00:26:39,550
That's exactly how you do bug fixing in event sourcing.

354
00:26:39,550 --> 00:26:51,190
If you want to know, what does the system look at a certain point in time, you can just build a new projection out of it, use exactly the events to reproduce this bug, and you know exactly what was the state when the customer clicked this button.

355
00:26:51,190 --> 00:26:54,390
And then you can just look, okay, the user clicked this button, the system was in that state.

356
00:26:54,390 --> 00:26:55,910
Well, that's clear, this is the problem.

357
00:26:55,910 --> 00:26:57,670
Very interesting way to work.

358
00:26:57,670 --> 00:26:59,870
And why is this so powerful?

359
00:26:59,870 --> 00:27:08,190
If you define everything in a table in the traditional architecture, you have just your customer's table, and all your use cases, they are using this customer's table.

360
00:27:08,190 --> 00:27:15,710
That creates a ton of coupling, and coupling is one of the biggest problems we face in our architectures, because now if you change one of those use cases,

361
00:27:15,710 --> 00:27:21,550
you have to retest all the other use cases, because they all depend on the same tables, they all depend on the same information.

362
00:27:21,550 --> 00:27:29,830
In event sourcing, this completely goes away, because what we're doing is we define one projection for each of those use cases.

363
00:27:29,830 --> 00:27:34,590
Every use case has its own customer's table with just the information that it needs. It doesn't need everything in this table.

364
00:27:34,600 --> 00:27:37,790
Typically, it's just a tiny fraction of this information.

365
00:27:37,790 --> 00:27:43,910
So, you have not one customer table, you have many small customer tables, specific for those use cases.

366
00:27:43,910 --> 00:27:48,350
And this gets rid of all those coupling problems, and it's an amazing way to define systems.

367
00:27:48,350 --> 00:28:00,870
And in terms of evolution of events, because I can imagine that over time, we want to change some information, maybe as example associated to a customer, maybe you want to change the format of the address, or add some more information.

368
00:28:00,880 --> 00:28:07,870
Basically, you have a situation where the events change in terms of what the information they carry.

369
00:28:07,870 --> 00:28:10,390
So, how do you manage this evolution?

370
00:28:10,390 --> 00:28:12,230
Yes, there are different ways how to deal with it.

371
00:28:12,230 --> 00:28:15,470
So, I always approach this in the same order.

372
00:28:15,470 --> 00:28:19,350
So, the first thing I typically check is if I need to change an event, because maybe there is a new field.

373
00:28:19,350 --> 00:28:23,630
Is this really just the structure change to the event, or is this maybe a new event?

374
00:28:23,640 --> 00:28:30,550
So, for example, if we need to add a field to the customer address, maybe this is like a postal code added event.

375
00:28:30,560 --> 00:28:36,910
Can I just add a new event to the system that basically brings this information to the existing event?

376
00:28:36,910 --> 00:28:40,390
So, I try to not change events if I don't have to. So, that's always the first thing I try.

377
00:28:40,400 --> 00:28:45,070
It's much easier to introduce new events, as long as they have real business meaning.

378
00:28:45,070 --> 00:28:52,070
So, I don't introduce new events just for to introduce new events, they need to be relevant to the business but if that is the case, that's my first choice always.

379
00:28:52,070 --> 00:28:59,910
But sometimes this is not the case so very very stupid example, there is a typo in in a field in an event. Do you want to keep that or do you want to change that?

380
00:28:59,910 --> 00:29:03,710
So sometimes you just you need to make structural changes to an event.

381
00:29:03,710 --> 00:29:12,830
And if that is the case, what you typically do is you just introduce a new version of this event so events are typically versioned so that would be then just a a customer registered V2,

382
00:29:12,830 --> 00:29:21,790
a second version of this event and then you can slowly and gradually adjust all the projections that depend on this event and then at some point in time you just work with this version two of the events.

383
00:29:21,790 --> 00:29:26,630
And if you need to process those older versions of an event,

384
00:29:26,630 --> 00:29:33,830
typically what you have is the concept is called an upcaster and that you basically take this first version of the event and there is a small piece of infrastructure that

385
00:29:33,830 --> 00:29:41,350
knows how to transform this version one into a version two so your system only from this this point on has to work with the version two of the event.

386
00:29:41,350 --> 00:29:44,830
That's typically how you deal with those things.

387
00:29:44,830 --> 00:29:51,950
Is there a risk that over time you will write a lot of software in your in your system to lots of upcasters if you like?

388
00:29:51,950 --> 00:29:58,670
Yeah, I mean of course that that can happen but in reality that is typically not the case so your business process they don't change over time and

389
00:29:58,680 --> 00:30:07,150
typically business process they are very stable and since what we are modeling and what we are discussing is the business process, they tend to be really really stable.

390
00:30:07,150 --> 00:30:19,630
It's very different if you look at it from a technical perspective because the technicalities in the project they change very quickly but the processes themselves they are typically very stable so yes in in theory that could happen in practice that is not what I see.

391
00:30:19,630 --> 00:30:24,792
Okay so in practice is less of a problem that what what it looks like. Yes. Basically.

392
00:30:24,800 --> 00:30:38,670
Yeah, I can understand this because if you established a ubiquitous language at the beginning basically you are if I understand correctly you are focusing a lot on the business so the business processes actually drive the shape of the data and the information.

393
00:30:38,670 --> 00:30:46,070
Since those processes change slowly, chances for this data to change are actually not that big at the end of the day.

394
00:30:46,080 --> 00:30:56,430
Instead, if we go the other way around, if we model the information without considering the business processes from the beginning, we may end up in a situation where we keep changing those much more frequently.

395
00:30:56,430 --> 00:30:58,111
Am I understanding correctly? Yes, absolutely.

396
00:30:58,111 --> 00:30:59,990
That's exactly the point.

397
00:30:59,990 --> 00:31:06,270
And what is also the point is those events, they are really happening at very specific points in the process.

398
00:31:06,270 --> 00:31:11,350
So, typically, if you refactor an existing database, often this has effects all over the place in the system typically.

399
00:31:11,350 --> 00:31:15,190
But, if you just change this one event, the effect is very, very small and very contained.

400
00:31:15,190 --> 00:31:21,310
So, even if you change one event, it typically doesn't affect all the other places in the system.

401
00:31:21,310 --> 00:31:24,710
So, changes in the system are very contained in in very small area.

402
00:31:24,710 --> 00:31:27,391
So, it's typically not a big problem. Okay.

403
00:31:27,391 --> 00:31:29,791
And let's talk a bit about adoption. Yeah?

