00:34:06,990 --> 00:34:12,590
That's a very interesting question and my opinion here is a little bit contrary to what most people in the industry are saying.

444
00:34:12,590 --> 00:34:20,470
Listen in the industry what people are saying, typically they say the default is CRUD and only if you have a very specific use case, you should use event sourcing.

445
00:34:20,470 --> 00:34:25,470
So event sourcing is not for everything, only use it if you have a very specific use case.

446
00:34:25,470 --> 00:34:31,030
For example, you are working a regulated industry, you have a lot of audits that you need to make, stuff like that, then you use event sourcing.

447
00:34:31,030 --> 00:34:33,430
My opinion is a completely different one.

448
00:34:33,430 --> 00:34:42,950
I say event sourcing is always the default because it's a simpler way to build system and only if there is a very, very specific reason, I would fall back to building a crap based system.

449
00:34:42,950 --> 00:34:53,270
So, the only reason I would build a crap based system is if I clearly have only a crap based application which is basically just a form with a lot of information that I need to store.

450
00:34:53,270 --> 00:35:01,910
But even then, the problem is those forms typically, sooner or later, also turn into real applications, into real business processes and then you would also benefit there from event sourcing.

451
00:35:01,910 --> 00:35:10,710
So, if you ask me, I would always use event sourcing and only in a very, very tiny fraction of systems I would fall back to crap.

452
00:35:10,710 --> 00:35:12,430
Okay, because I was also thinking, you know, thinking with differently.

453
00:35:12,430 --> 00:35:14,670
So, to me, tell me if I'm wrong, okay?

454
00:35:14,670 --> 00:35:19,750
So, to me seems that it's mostly around enterprise applications, you know, when we model business processes.

455
00:35:19,750 --> 00:35:31,230
But for example, developers that work in the space of more embedded systems or things like this, I guess they for those kind of systems is not necessarily good idea to think about that.

456
00:35:31,230 --> 00:35:32,790
Yes, that really depends.

457
00:35:32,790 --> 00:35:42,670
So, algorithmic systems, like if you use operating systems, very low-level stuff, embedded systems, they of course you need to look is event sourcing the real thing.

458
00:35:42,670 --> 00:35:47,431
So, where event sourcing really shines is everything about information management. So, Okay.

459
00:35:47,431 --> 00:35:54,470
the typical systems you see in in the enterprise, you put in information, this gets transformed, you have many ways how this information is used.

460
00:35:54,470 --> 00:36:00,190
If you need to track how information is used along a single timeline, that's typically where event sourcing really shines.

461
00:36:00,190 --> 00:36:07,952
If you use algorithms and stuff like that, of course, that might not be the best use case. Okay. Okay.

462
00:36:07,952 --> 00:36:09,790
In terms of trade-offs that the team should consider.

463
00:36:09,790 --> 00:36:16,950
So, between event source architecture and let's say and call it a more traditional one, yeah?

464
00:36:16,950 --> 00:36:28,110
But you say say instead of event sourcing we use let's say a relational database or well, maybe even not the necessary relational, but without storing events as we do with event sourcing, yeah?

465
00:36:28,110 --> 00:36:32,270
So, are there any trade-offs there in that we need to consider for this choice?

466
00:36:32,270 --> 00:36:38,830
You know, I don't know anything in terms of could be performances, could be space on this, could be anything.

467
00:36:38,830 --> 00:36:40,510
Yes, of course, there are a few.

468
00:36:40,510 --> 00:36:46,470
So, the first thing you need to consider when you think about adopting event sourcing is it's a very different mindset you need.

469
00:36:46,470 --> 00:36:51,990
So, you cannot just tell your your developers, "From today on, we will be doing event sourcing and everything will be fine."

470
00:36:52,000 --> 00:36:53,510
That is typically doomed to fail.

471
00:36:53,510 --> 00:36:55,590
That will not work.

472
00:36:55,590 --> 00:36:57,950
So, the first thing you need is you need the right mindset.

473
00:36:57,950 --> 00:36:59,790
And uh this takes quite a while.

474
00:36:59,790 --> 00:37:08,990
It also took me almost 2 years to get rid of all those ideas I learned in the past and um build systems the way I build systems today using slices.

475
00:37:08,990 --> 00:37:10,550
It's a very different thing.

476
00:37:10,550 --> 00:37:14,190
You cannot just tell your developers to do it from now on and it will work.

477
00:37:14,190 --> 00:37:16,190
Most probably, that is doomed to fail.

478
00:37:16,190 --> 00:37:17,790
So, it takes a while to get into this mindset.

479
00:37:17,790 --> 00:37:21,910
And the hardest part with this is typically really unlearning what you have learned.

480
00:37:21,910 --> 00:37:32,510
So, building systems like this requires you to unlearn quite a few things you might think of as best practices today, which in event sourcing are simply no longer best practices.

481
00:37:32,510 --> 00:37:34,470
They are rather problems you you try to prevent.

482
00:37:34,470 --> 00:37:42,110
But, of course, also other trade-offs might be you need to build up the right infrastructure to do event sourcing.

483
00:37:42,110 --> 00:37:44,190
And this doesn't have to be an commercial product.

484
00:37:44,190 --> 00:37:47,950
So, you can start very small, like with a Postgres database using a relational database.

485
00:37:47,950 --> 00:37:52,070
I have a lot of clients that simply use a Postgres database as an event store.

486
00:37:52,070 --> 00:37:56,270
Or some even use a Microsoft MS SQL Server as an event store.

487
00:37:56,270 --> 00:37:58,070
That is perfectly fine.

488
00:37:58,070 --> 00:38:00,510
So, typically, the the entry barrier is very low.

489
00:38:00,510 --> 00:38:13,070
But, of course, to do this right, also if it comes to scale, if you build bigger systems, you need to consider quite a few things because even a Postgres database, which is really, really powerful, if you have enough events in your system, it becomes slow.

490
00:38:13,070 --> 00:38:14,670
There's something you need to consider.

491
00:38:14,670 --> 00:38:17,910
And then oftentimes, you need to evaluate, does it make sense to have a commercial product?

492
00:38:17,910 --> 00:38:20,990
And there are quite a few commercial products available to do that.

493
00:38:20,990 --> 00:38:24,310
Especially in the beginning, that the entry barrier is really, really low.

494
00:38:24,310 --> 00:38:26,110
And many systems will never reach that scale.

495
00:38:26,110 --> 00:38:33,070
Most systems are rather small, so most systems don't reach that scale where where your database will get into trouble.

496
00:38:33,070 --> 00:38:34,910
But some systems do, and then you need to consider what to do with it.

497
00:38:34,920 --> 00:38:36,830
Okay.

498
00:38:36,830 --> 00:38:48,870
And now, going back to the unlearning, what kind of things developers need to unlearn that that are maybe best practices with their previous approach to software development that are not really best practices when using event sourcing?

499
00:38:48,880 --> 00:38:56,390
Especially at the combination of event sourcing and uh slice-based architectures require a completely different thinking.

500
00:38:56,390 --> 00:39:01,190
And the one thing I struggled with the most was reuse, code reuse.

501
00:39:01,200 --> 00:39:04,751
It's basically it's non-existent in my projects. I don't have any code reuse.

502
00:39:04,760 --> 00:39:08,710
Because when you work with those slices, you keep those slices independent.

503
00:39:08,720 --> 00:39:11,310
They're very small, but they're completely independent.

504
00:39:11,310 --> 00:39:14,750
And what you can't do is there is no code reuse between those slices.

505
00:39:14,750 --> 00:39:17,230
So, you cannot just have any abstract classes.

506
00:39:17,230 --> 00:39:23,150
I I didn't use any abstract classes in 5 years or so. I I never used that because it creates a lot of coupling.

507
00:39:23,160 --> 00:39:34,230
So, the whole DRY principles, don't repeat yourself, for most developers that means, oh, I I have functionality implemented in one part of the system, and I will just reuse it from everywhere.

508
00:39:34,240 --> 00:39:40,230
But this creates a lot of coupling. And in a slice-based architecture, that doesn't work because you want those slices to be independent.

509
00:39:40,230 --> 00:39:43,870
So, what you do is oftentimes is you copy information.

510
00:39:43,870 --> 00:39:45,670
You copy code between those slices.

511
00:39:45,670 --> 00:39:48,310
And it's perfectly fine to do that.

512
00:39:48,310 --> 00:39:50,391
So, building slice-based systems means a lot of copying. You copy-paste.

513
00:39:50,400 --> 00:39:51,950
I'm extremely good at copy-pasting.

514
00:39:51,950 --> 00:39:56,270
And nowadays, copy-paste means generating with AI.

515
00:39:56,270 --> 00:39:57,830
That's That's just copy copy and paste on steroids.

516
00:39:57,830 --> 00:40:00,510
It's the same thing.

517
00:40:00,510 --> 00:40:02,550
Many developers really, really struggle with this.

518
00:40:02,550 --> 00:40:04,310
Actually, now they say, "Okay, we'll copy and paste."

519
00:40:04,310 --> 00:40:12,790
But does this create issues like you have to change some logic, and then you have to change the same logic in all the places where you did the copy and paste?

520
00:40:12,790 --> 00:40:19,750
Or the copy and paste is actually in this particular context is not affected by these problems.

521
00:40:19,750 --> 00:40:30,510
Of course, that can happen, but what typically happens is since we model the system first with the event modeling, typically this logic isn't spread out throughout the whole system. Typically, you have one slice that takes care of this.

522
00:40:30,510 --> 00:40:37,950
Of course, there can be something that is copied to a few other slices, so can happen that you need to adjust two or three projections, for example, if you change an event.

523
00:40:37,950 --> 00:40:42,230
That that's perfectly fine, that can happen, but it's not that bad as it sounds.

524
00:40:42,230 --> 00:40:44,750
So, typically, logic in the system is very contained in slices.

525
00:40:44,760 --> 00:40:46,550
That's what I'm seeing again and again.

526
00:40:46,560 --> 00:40:59,710
And also, I really completely lost any fear of copying also because of AI, because AI can tell you very, very well, "Okay, if you change this, those two or three other places also need to be adjusted."

527
00:40:59,710 --> 00:41:02,230
So, if you ask me that that is really no longer a problem at all.

528
00:41:02,240 --> 00:41:05,150
Mhm.

529
00:41:05,150 --> 00:41:10,310
Okay, so when rolling out this event sourcing, so let's say my company that has never done it before, yeah?

530
00:41:10,310 --> 00:41:15,070
So, first of all, well, we said the technical trade-offs, but what about the business trade-offs?

531
00:41:15,080 --> 00:41:24,910
So, why should a company consider, you know, moving whatever systems they have into a different design using event event sourcing?

532
00:41:24,910 --> 00:41:27,950
What kind of advantages they can get out of doing this?

533
00:41:27,960 --> 00:41:29,910
Yes.

534
00:41:29,910 --> 00:41:40,470
The biggest advantage, and that was also the reason why I initially went into that direction, slice-based architecture event sourcing, is whenever you work on a traditional architecture, in the beginning it's it's easy.

535
00:41:40,470 --> 00:41:42,070
It's no problem.

536
00:41:42,070 --> 00:41:44,030
That's also same idea with the greenfield project.

537
00:41:44,030 --> 00:41:47,590
When you start, it's very easy because there are not a lot of features.

538
00:41:47,590 --> 00:41:50,950
You can implement those features in isolation typically, and everything is fine.

539
00:41:50,950 --> 00:41:56,670
But the longer you work in those systems, the more those features have cross-dependencies, the more they depend on each other.

540
00:41:56,670 --> 00:41:59,350
What we're talking about, of course, is coupling.

541
00:41:59,350 --> 00:42:02,230
The longer you work in those projects, the more coupling accumulates.

542
00:42:02,240 --> 00:42:05,870
If you don't invest heavily to get rid of that.

543
00:42:05,870 --> 00:42:11,790
So, if you don't do constant refactorings and uh keep the coupling very contained, coupling just grows in the system.

544
00:42:11,790 --> 00:42:19,990
And this means those systems they get slower in terms of development speed, and they get more expensive because in the beginning you build one feature and you're good,

545
00:42:19,990 --> 00:42:26,910
but later in the project you build one feature, but then you also need to adjust those five other features because they all depend on each other.

546
00:42:26,910 --> 00:42:30,630
That's what makes what makes late-stage development very, very expensive.

547
00:42:30,630 --> 00:42:33,870
So the longer you work on those projects, the more expensive it gets.

548
00:42:33,870 --> 00:42:35,750
And this is where almost all companies just struggle.

549
00:42:35,760 --> 00:42:39,670
That's a problem that haunts us for two decades already and much longer.

550
00:42:39,670 --> 00:42:41,870
And that I never found a solution to this.

551
00:42:41,880 --> 00:42:45,590
So my my whole career I was struggling with this.

552
00:42:45,590 --> 00:42:47,430
How can we do this the right way?

553
00:42:47,430 --> 00:42:51,950
And it was really hard, always hard, until I started to work with slices.

554
00:42:51,950 --> 00:42:55,710
Because if you work with slices, any new feature you throw at the system, it doesn't matter which one.

555
00:42:55,710 --> 00:42:58,350
Any new feature is typically just a new slice.

556
00:42:58,360 --> 00:43:02,911
So your architectures, they have a flat cost curve. The cost curve is very flat.

557
00:43:02,920 --> 00:43:06,270
You don't have this hockey stick exponential growing cost curve.

558
00:43:06,270 --> 00:43:08,270
It's very flat.

559
00:43:08,270 --> 00:43:12,630
No matter what requirements you add to the system, you just add new slices.

560
00:43:12,630 --> 00:43:14,550
It's almost like an append-only architecture.

561
00:43:14,550 --> 00:43:20,390
Just adding new slices, which means since those slices are independent from each other, you don't touch them.

562
00:43:20,390 --> 00:43:25,190
They almost behave like small, tiny little microservices within your system.

563
00:43:25,190 --> 00:43:31,790
You only touch those slices if the requirements change, and that is such a beautiful way of working because you never have to look at uh the whole code base.

564
00:43:31,790 --> 00:43:36,270
You never have to look at, "Okay, what did I break now by this change?"

565
00:43:36,270 --> 00:43:38,550
Because change is only for one slice and one slice only.

566
00:43:38,550 --> 00:43:44,910
This is really it's a great way to develop systems because your time to market with this is just amazing.

567
00:43:44,910 --> 00:43:53,350
And what's also amazing is if you work with AI, and I mean every company nowadays tries to find good ways to work with AI, your coding agent never has to understand the whole code base.

568
00:43:53,360 --> 00:43:55,030
It just has to understand one slice.

569
00:43:55,030 --> 00:43:58,390
And this might be just five classes, for example.

570
00:43:58,390 --> 00:44:05,870
So you never need this uh 1 million token context window because typically the tasks you use for your coding agents, they are very, very small.

571
00:44:05,870 --> 00:44:10,950
So it's a very good way to to integrate AI into the development process.

572
00:44:10,950 --> 00:44:18,430
Okay, and since you mentioned before that it is actually difficult to get started with it for developers and have to change the mindset and everything.

573
00:44:18,430 --> 00:44:24,830
So, then a company that decides to go in that direction, how would you recommend them them to get started?

574
00:44:24,830 --> 00:44:27,430
You know, training developers or hire the right people?

575
00:44:27,440 --> 00:44:35,230
What is that your suggestion for a company that decides to go into this direction? The first step is always even modeling.

576
00:44:35,230 --> 00:44:37,710
So, I wouldn't start with event sourcing.

577
00:44:37,710 --> 00:44:46,870
The precondition for event sourcing is some way of collaborative modeling and my choice is always even modeling because it for me it's the simplest way, but also event storming, all those methodologies help.

578
00:44:46,880 --> 00:44:48,590
There's also domain storytelling.

579
00:44:48,590 --> 00:44:52,030
They are all wonderful all wonderful methodologies.

580
00:44:52,030 --> 00:44:53,950
Just my my choice is even modeling because it it it fits so well.

581
00:44:53,960 --> 00:44:59,670
So, the first thing I would always recommend is start by modeling, start by understanding your business processes.

582
00:44:59,680 --> 00:45:01,470
Don't think about technology.

583
00:45:01,470 --> 00:45:03,070
Start by understanding your business process.

584
00:45:03,070 --> 00:45:07,230
You can even do this for your current database systems.

585
00:45:07,230 --> 00:45:09,070
You don't have to do event sourcing to start with modeling.

586
00:45:09,080 --> 00:45:14,911
You get the all the benefits of even modeling today with your existing system. That that's perfectly fine.

587
00:45:14,920 --> 00:45:24,390
But then if you started to model those systems and you really understand those processes, there's a natural way to transition from an event model to an event source system.

588
00:45:24,390 --> 00:45:31,830
So, if you do did the modeling correct and you you trained your people on doing this, this can be by workshops, this can be just by bringing in people who help.

589
00:45:31,830 --> 00:45:37,110
There are many ways to do this or just give them my book to read. That's also a good starting point.

590
00:45:37,110 --> 00:45:39,070
So, there are many ways to to get started.

591
00:45:39,070 --> 00:45:41,110
And even modeling is really it's a simple methodology.

592
00:45:41,110 --> 00:45:43,350
You getting started is really really simple.

593
00:45:43,360 --> 00:45:48,671
But when they master that, that's already the first step to change this mindset. The mindset changes.

594
00:45:48,671 --> 00:45:50,510
People start to think about processes.

595
00:45:50,510 --> 00:45:55,030
They don't no longer think about databases and tables and microservices.

596
00:45:55,030 --> 00:45:58,550
They start to think about processes and this changes a lot.

597
00:45:58,550 --> 00:46:05,750
And what you typically do then is if you have to see the model, you just have to look at the event model and you see what would be a good candidate.

598
00:46:05,750 --> 00:46:08,390
Can Is there something we can extract from our legacy system?

599
00:46:08,390 --> 00:46:10,430
Is there Oh, there's a small process here.

600
00:46:10,430 --> 00:46:12,030
How about we make this a new microservice?

601
00:46:12,040 --> 00:46:13,910
And we just make a proof of concept.

602
00:46:13,910 --> 00:46:15,590
You typically start with a proof of concept.

603
00:46:15,600 --> 00:46:20,510
You don't do a big bang and then say, "From today on, everything is event sourcing."

604
00:46:20,510 --> 00:46:22,230
That's typically not what happens.

605
00:46:22,230 --> 00:46:24,311
You start with a small proof of concept. One tiny little process.

606
00:46:24,311 --> 00:46:27,110
Let's build a microservice using event sourcing.

607
00:46:27,110 --> 00:46:28,750
Let's build a blueprint architecture.

608
00:46:28,750 --> 00:46:32,910
How would a blueprint architecture for us look like? What's the stack we are using? There are many different stacks.

609
00:46:32,910 --> 00:46:35,110
If it's.NET, you might use Martin and Wolverine.

610
00:46:35,110 --> 00:46:37,710
If it's on the JVM, typically might be Axon.

611
00:46:37,720 --> 00:46:41,070
There are many different ways how to get started.

612
00:46:41,070 --> 00:46:43,950
Or many companies start to build their own frameworks, which is also fine.

613
00:46:43,950 --> 00:46:46,110
So, very easy to get started typically.

614
00:46:46,110 --> 00:46:56,390
Of course, when it comes to scale, you might want to use an established framework, but just for our first proof of concept, it's perfectly fine to build your own event sourcing framework, and it's fun.

615
00:46:56,400 --> 00:47:00,950
Let's Let's face it, it's fun to build your own framework, so why not invest a little bit of time with AI?

616
00:47:00,950 --> 00:47:04,590
This can be done in 1 or 2 days, so it's it's really not a big thing anymore.

617
00:47:04,590 --> 00:47:13,750
And then if you have this first proof of concept that is running, maybe even in production, what you will realize is you achieved a lot in a very short period of time.

618
00:47:13,750 --> 00:47:21,550
So, it's it's crazy how fast you can build those systems if you combine event modeling, event sourcing, and the slice-based architecture. Within 2 days, you can have a system running in production.

619
00:47:21,560 --> 00:47:25,150
It's crazy how fast you can go with this.

620
00:47:25,150 --> 00:47:31,110
And typically, those systems, since they are built together by business and engineering in collaboration, they are exactly what those people need.

621
00:47:31,110 --> 00:47:33,270
So, typically, they are very successful in production.

622
00:47:33,270 --> 00:47:35,110
You mentioned legacy system.

623
00:47:35,110 --> 00:47:41,070
So, with a legacy system, then I guess that you would start with maybe you said start with event modeling.

624
00:47:41,070 --> 00:47:46,991
So, I would imagine part of that would be modeling what the system currently does, at least for the Yes. Parts that are interesting.

625
00:47:46,991 --> 00:47:48,670
And then what would you do?

626
00:47:48,670 --> 00:47:58,270
Then you choose where to start as in you said creating a prototype or some microservices or something else to start to change the legacy system into the new shape.

627
00:47:58,270 --> 00:47:59,830
Yes, that's typically what happens.

628
00:47:59,830 --> 00:48:12,190
So, in the last months I support many clients in modernizing legacy systems and what I'm basically doing is I'm using AI to analyze existing systems and build event models from this basically automatically.

629
00:48:12,190 --> 00:48:16,430
And this works really well and I'm I'm doing this from two sides typically.

630
00:48:16,430 --> 00:48:21,830
So, I use AI agents to analyze the existing code base and I use AI agents to go through the UI.

631
00:48:21,830 --> 00:48:28,950
So, if if there is a UI, you can just use them to go through the UI and basically build the event models from the UI just to understand the processes.

632
00:48:28,950 --> 00:48:33,111
And you can also automatically take those screenshots, put them into the event model. It's quite interesting.

633
00:48:33,111 --> 00:48:43,830
What you're basically doing is you gather a lot of information about the system because what most companies struggle with is they have those big gigantic systems that make a lot of money typically,

634
00:48:43,830 --> 00:48:51,030
but they don't really know how they work because the this knowledge is retired or it's gone to other companies, it's no longer there.

635
00:48:51,030 --> 00:48:56,550
So, they have a lot of information in those systems, but they don't know how that works.

636
00:48:56,550 --> 00:49:06,830
And if you ask me, before you do any modernization, any code, before you write the first line of code for any modernization, the first thing you need to do is completely understand how does the system work. You cannot modernize what you don't understand.

637
00:49:06,830 --> 00:49:09,750
That is doomed to fail. So, you really need to understand how that works.

638
00:49:09,750 --> 00:49:19,350
And using event modeling for that is is a very good step because you bring all those people together and you can extract the knowledge that is available, use AI to fill in the gaps. That works really really well.

639
00:49:19,350 --> 00:49:22,510
And instead for a greenfield system, can you have an approach that is kind of incremental?

640
00:49:22,520 --> 00:49:32,830
So, what I mean is maybe you have some event modeling workshop, but then you are not quite sure about the entire system or the direction and but you know some initial parts.

641
00:49:32,830 --> 00:49:36,110
So, can you actually do this thing in a incremental iterative manner?

642
00:49:36,120 --> 00:49:38,151
Yes. Yes, absolutely.

643
00:49:38,151 --> 00:49:43,190
So, what we typically do is we start to model, but we don't model the whole system and then we start.

644
00:49:43,190 --> 00:49:48,790
What we typically do is we model the system on a very high level, then we pick one tiny workflow that we are pretty sure about, that we understand.

645
00:49:48,790 --> 00:49:51,151
Okay, let's take the registration. We know how the our works.

646
00:49:51,160 --> 00:49:54,190
Let's start with that. And maybe it's just five slices.

647
00:49:54,200 --> 00:49:55,950
Five slices is enough to get started.

648
00:49:55,960 --> 00:50:04,830
Write some code, build a proof of concept, bring it to pre-production or even to production, it doesn't really matter. This piece of the software is already done.

649
00:50:04,830 --> 00:50:08,270
And then we can decide, okay, based on the event model, uh what is the next process we want to tackle?

650
00:50:08,280 --> 00:50:09,790
Where do we have the most knowledge now?

651
00:50:09,800 --> 00:50:12,910
Where are the least gaps? And then we might do that.

652
00:50:12,910 --> 00:50:15,070
So, you can build it very, very incrementally, step by step.

653
00:50:15,080 --> 00:50:18,150
Even if you want slice by slice, that's also possible.

654
00:50:18,150 --> 00:50:20,150
So, yes, that that's typically the way to go.

655
00:50:20,150 --> 00:50:23,910
And this also gives you the possibility to change directions.

656
00:50:23,910 --> 00:50:28,230
So, you don't have to have those long project plans and the plan the whole migration.

657
00:50:28,240 --> 00:50:29,790
You can go very small, step by step.

658
00:50:29,790 --> 00:50:37,030
And if you if you find out, well, this goes in a very wrong direction, you can always change directions, because it's slice by slice.

659
00:50:37,030 --> 00:50:39,150
You can basically change on a daily basis, if you want.

660
00:50:39,150 --> 00:50:49,750
And in terms of testability, in terms of, you know, testing, unit testing, various levels of testing, are there any differences between event sourcing systems and more traditional ones?

661
00:50:49,760 --> 00:50:59,470
So, what I'm using heavily, really, really heavily, is behavior-driven development, and that's a very good combination together with event sourcing systems.

662
00:50:59,470 --> 00:51:03,670
So, basically, we do this on a slice by slice basis, and you do this also already in event modeling.

663
00:51:03,670 --> 00:51:06,510
You define basically, what are the acceptance criteria?

664
00:51:06,510 --> 00:51:11,510
What are the business rules for one slice, using given-when-then?

665
00:51:11,510 --> 00:51:14,230
It's just Let's make an example, and maybe that's the the easiest thing.

666
00:51:14,230 --> 00:51:22,270
Let's say we have this customer registration, and there's just a rule, we only allow customers with the same uh surname once in the system.

667
00:51:22,270 --> 00:51:34,030
You would have a given event, given there is already a customer with the surname, when I try to register this customer again, or another customer with the same surname, then this might would be an exception, for example.

668
00:51:34,030 --> 00:51:37,470
So, you describe all those business rules for each slice in the system.

669
00:51:37,480 --> 00:51:46,750
What I do is I use the given-when-then specifications from the event model, and I generate executable specifications in code.

670
00:51:46,750 --> 00:51:52,110
And this is such a great way of working, because those specifications in the event model are defined by the business people.

671
00:51:52,110 --> 00:51:53,790
So, this is the real business rules.

672
00:51:53,790 --> 00:51:57,230
You're not talking about technical unit tested test things that are not important.

673
00:51:57,230 --> 00:52:00,430
We are testing the real business rules that are defined by the business people.

674
00:52:00,430 --> 00:52:09,750
And if you translate them to executable specifications and all those specifications are green, there's a very high chance that the system does exactly what it needs to do.

675
00:52:09,760 --> 00:52:12,711
Okay. And now a final question actually.

676
00:52:12,720 --> 00:52:25,830
So, have you got any suggestion for metrics or signals or anything that can be used to demonstrate that, you know, using an event modeling well, an event sourcing system is actually improving delivery?

677
00:52:25,830 --> 00:52:28,990
Because you see, a company that invests on it say, "Okay, we want to invest on this thing.

678
00:52:28,990 --> 00:52:41,830
We have a system, an existing one, we want to go into this direction instead which seems to be better, but what kind of metrics they should look at to decide that actually changing into an event sourcing system they have a good return on investment?"

679
00:52:41,840 --> 00:52:46,150
Yeah, so what I use as a measurement is really I call it the slice cycle time.

680
00:52:46,160 --> 00:52:52,390
So, the slice cycle time is basically how long does it take a team to build one slice?

681
00:52:52,390 --> 00:52:57,790
So, what we typically do is we start with a with an event modeling workshop that lasts typically for 3 days.

682
00:52:57,790 --> 00:53:01,070
We do 2 days of event modeling, modeling a process, and the third day is typically building the system.

683
00:53:01,070 --> 00:53:04,110
What we start to do there is we define the blueprint architecture.

684
00:53:04,110 --> 00:53:06,910
What does a slice look like for the company?

685
00:53:06,910 --> 00:53:10,350
And then what you start to measure is the slice cycle time.

686
00:53:10,350 --> 00:53:11,870
How long does it take your team to build a slice?

687
00:53:11,870 --> 00:53:21,990
In the beginning this will typically be around two or three days maybe with AI that's much shorter, but what you measure is how long does it does it take my team to build a slice?

688
00:53:21,990 --> 00:53:26,110
And if you do this long enough, what you will realize is this cycle time will get smaller and smaller.

689
00:53:26,120 --> 00:53:29,550
So, in the end it might just be half a day for example to build a full slice.

690
00:53:29,560 --> 00:53:31,950
And this includes the UI.

691
00:53:31,950 --> 00:53:33,790
That's a whole piece of functionality.

692
00:53:33,790 --> 00:53:37,230
And what you then do is those slices they are production ready if you do it right.

693
00:53:37,230 --> 00:53:43,750
So, you can build those small pieces of functionality and you can continuously deliver those slices to production.

694
00:53:43,750 --> 00:53:48,950
That means your time to production will be extremely short.

695
00:53:48,950 --> 00:53:52,110
So, you you can deliver multiple times a day working software to production.

696
00:53:52,110 --> 00:53:54,950
Of course, that also works with other methodologies.

697
00:53:54,950 --> 00:54:02,030
Event sourcing is not the only thing, but a team that starts to work like this typically increases the deliveries to production massively.

698
00:54:02,030 --> 00:54:07,832
And that's that's typically what I try companies to really adopt and where they should go to. Okay. Thank you.

699
00:54:07,832 --> 00:54:09,910
I think we have come to the end of the episode.

700
